\description{
Splits the variation in a community among two to four sets of explanatory
variables (such as environment, space, and time): the fractions that each
set explains on its own, the fractions they explain jointly, and what
none of them explains. Each fraction is an adjusted \eqn{R^2} from
(partial) redundancy analysis of a count table (\code{\link[=rda]{rda()}}), distance-based
redundancy analysis of distances (\code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}), or canonical correspondence
analysis (\code{\link[=cca]{cca()}}), as in \code{vegan::varpart()}, with the same results.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

For each set and each union of sets, the constrained ordination of \code{x}
on that set's model columns gives \eqn{R^2}, the fraction of the total
inertia it explains, and its adjusted \eqn{R^2}. For RDA and db-RDA that
is Ezekiel's adjustment, \eqn{1 - (1 - R^2)(n - 1)/(n - m - 1)} for
\eqn{n} samples and \eqn{m} model columns (the rank); for CCA it is
estimated by permutation, as \code{\link[=cca]{cca()}} does (Peres-Neto et al. 2006). The
fractions of the Venn diagram are then sums and differences of those
values: with two sets X1 and X2, \eqn{[a] = R^2_{12} - R^2_2},
\eqn{[b] = R^2_{12} - R^2_1}, \eqn{[c] = R^2_1 + R^2_2 - R^2_{12}}, and
the residuals \eqn{[d] = 1 - R^2_{12}}; with more sets, by inclusion and
exclusion. Model columns that are linear combinations of others (within
or between sets) are dropped from each union, as \code{qr()} decides with a
tolerance of \code{1e-7}, so shared columns move into the shared fractions.

Only the unique fractions can be tested, each by a partial constrained
ordination of the set given all the others, as with \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}} on
\code{x}'s distances and \code{formula = ~ set + Condition(others)}.

\strong{Speed}

For RDA and db-RDA, one pass over the table's non-zeros (or the
distances) gives the cross products of all the sets' columns, from
which every union's \eqn{R^2} follows without another pass, so the cost
is about that of a single \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}} or \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} of all the sets together.
No \eqn{n \times n}{n x n} matrix is formed, and sparse tables stay
sparse. For CCA, each of the \eqn{2^k - 1} unions of \eqn{k} sets is a
\code{\link[=cca]{cca()}} with its permutations. Results are identical for any number of
\code{cpus}.

\strong{Differences from vegan::varpart()}
\itemize{
\item The result is one data frame of the Venn fractions, with vegan's
other tables as attributes; there is no plot.
\item \code{r_squared} is given for the Venn fractions too.
\item \code{df} is \code{NA}, not \code{0}, for shared fractions and the residuals.
\item Normalizations are ecodive's (\code{norm}), not \code{decostand()}'s
\code{transfo}; there is no \code{scale}, and no \code{add} for distances.
\item Samples are matched by name between \code{x}, \code{data}, and the sets.
}

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::varpart()} has the same name. When both packages are attached,
the one attached last masks the other; call \code{ecodive::varpart()} to be
sure.
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # 40 samples: two environmental variables, and coordinates.
    set.seed(1)
    meta <- data.frame(
      ph   = runif(40, 5, 8),
      temp = runif(40, 10, 20),
      x    = runif(40),
      y    = runif(40) )
    counts <- matrix(rpois(40 * 15, 4), 40, 15)
    counts[, 1:5] <- counts[, 1:5] + round(3 * meta$ph + 4 * meta$x)

    # tb-RDA on the Hellinger-transformed table.
    vp <- varpart(
      counts, env = ~ ph + temp, space = ~ x + y,
      data = meta, norm = 'hellinger' )
    vp
    plot(vp)

    # db-RDA on Bray-Curtis distances; a set may be a matrix.
    varpart(bray(counts), ~ ph + temp, as.matrix(meta[, c('x', 'y')]), data = meta)

    # Test the fraction unique to `env`.
    permanova(
      euclidean(counts, norm = 'hellinger'),
      data = meta, formula = ~ ph + temp + Condition(x + y) )

}
